window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_b1-2767756a35509b1a"]={"key":"ny_b1-2767756a35509b1a","group":"ny_b1","uid":"2767756a35509b1a","rank":94,"title":"cavendish overtime scheduling pilot protocol","occupation":"project_management_specialists","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_b1","prompt":"You’re Mikkel Fairbairn, the project management specialist at Cavendish Logistics, and Eamon Rasmussen has authorized you to set the operating protocol and planning budget for a pilot of a crew-scheduling tool before anyone starts using it. The vendor says the tool cuts overtime by 20%. Cavendish has eleven field crews supporting Emberline Clinic work in England and Scotland, but those crews differ in headcount, region, job mix, travel burden, and current overtime. Overtime also moves sharply with the season. Eamon will fund a pilot across only some crews and wants a design that can show either that the tool reduced overtime or that it did not provide decision-worthy evidence. Astrid Yamamoto in operations wants participation to be voluntary because adoption will be difficult otherwise, while the crews most likely to volunteer are already the best-run. There is a further edge case to settle: Astrid wants a replacement crew brought in if a selected crew is diverted to urgent client work, but Camila Okonkwo, contracting counsel, does not want the comparison changed once outcomes begin to emerge. We have also previously seen a raw before-and-after fall in overtime presented as proof of a scheduling initiative even though demand and season had changed, and this protocol must make that misuse hard to repeat. Prepare `crew_scheduling_pilot_protocol.pptx` for Eamon, Astrid, Camila, and Jasmin Ellingsen in finance. Write it into the top level of the working directory using exactly that filename. Keep it to no more than ten slides, including the title slide, and make the visible slides stand alone without relying on speaker notes. The protocol needs to be usable by the implementation team without coming back to you for design decisions. In one coherent presentation: (1) define the pilot question, scope, comparison, crew eligibility, allocation approach, timing, and the conditions that must be fixed or recorded so that seasonal demand, crew differences, volunteer bias, and operational disruptions do not become alternative explanations; (2) state exactly how overtime and any balancing measures will be calculated, what information must be captured, how tool use and departures from the protocol will be monitored, and how missing or unreliable data will be handled; (3) set an analysis and decision framework that distinguishes a meaningful result from ordinary variation, deals honestly with the small number of crews, and produces a clear interpretation for success, failure, harm, or an inconclusive result rather than treating the vendor’s 20% claim as a foregone conclusion; (4) settle the volunteer, withdrawal, diversion, replacement, and access-to-software cases before results are visible, including who can approve an exception and how it is documented; (5) provide an executable timeline with owners, readiness and stop gates, the final decision point, and the records needed to show whether the pilot was run as designed; (6) compare the implementation and contracting checks applicable to crews in England and Scotland, identifying what is common across both and what requires jurisdiction-specific confirmation, without presenting unsettled legal assumptions as Camila’s advice; (7) give a low, base, and high planning estimate in GBP covering the full pilot cost, with visible quantities, rates or allowances, arithmetic, assumptions, exclusions, and cost exposure if the pilot pauses or ends early; and (8) identify the contract protections needed before pilot access is granted, including treatment of crew and scheduling data and any commitment created by moving beyond the pilot. There are no source files, so use defensible planning assumptions and label them plainly rather than manufacturing historical crew performance, vendor quotations, legal conclusions, or statistical certainty.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `crew_scheduling_pilot_protocol.pptx` — A decision-ready pilot design protocol and low/base/high planning budget for the crew-scheduling tool. (Write the file at the top level of the working directory using exactly the specified filename.; Use no more than ten slides, including the title slide.; All critical content must be visible on the slides rather than confined to speaker notes.; Show budget scenarios in GBP.; Use labeled planning assumptions and do not invent historical performance, vendor quotations, or legal advice.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/crew_scheduling_pilot_protocol.pptx","bytes":53219,"extension":".pptx","url":"assets/c6bf1681dcd871ad97909dc3ecf13f24b2fb16d23b59326c34111282ef6f6b77.pptx","sha256":"c6bf1681dcd871ad97909dc3ecf13f24b2fb16d23b59326c34111282ef6f6b77","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"=== 幻灯片 1 ===\nCAVENDISH LOGISTICS  /  DECISION PACK\nCrew scheduling\npilot protocol\nA controlled, concurrent test for 11 Emberline Clinic field crews in England & Scotland\nOWNER  Mikkel Fairbairn\nSPONSOR  Eamon Rasmussen\nFor Eamon • Astrid Yamamoto • Camila Okonkwo • Jasmin Ellingsen\nDecision gate: approve design, funding and contract protections before any pilot access\n\n=== 幻灯片 2 ===\n01 / QUESTION & SCOPE\nWhat the pilot is allowed to answer\nCavendish Logistics • Emberline Clinic • planning assumption set • 20 Aug 2026\n02\nDecision question\nFor eligible crews, does access to and use of the scheduling tool reduce overtime per productive workload unit versus concurrent crews without access during the same 12-week season?\nVendor’s “20%” is a claim to test—not an expected result or pass mark.\nIn scope\nAll 11 Cavendish field crews serving Emberline Clinic work in England and Scotland. Assignment unit = whole crew; workers, dispatch and schedules can spill over.\nPilot access to 5–6 crews; retain 5–6 concurrent comparison crews.\nDesign guardrails frozen before outcome review\n• No raw before→after claim as primary evidence; demand and season move together.\n• Concurrent windows and a crew-and-time model are mandatory.\n• Volunteer willingness ≠ allocation: opt-in to training/notice, then balanced draw.\n• Record region, capacity/headcount, job mix, travel burden, baseline overtime and disruptions.\n• No post-result exclusions, replacements or subgroup shopping.\n• Primary population remains crews as assigned (intention-to-treat).\n\n=== 幻灯片 3 ===\n02 / ELIGIBILITY, ALLOCATION & TIMING\nFreeze the comparison before launch\nCavendish Logistics • Emberline Clinic • planning assumption set • 20 Aug 2026\n03\nEligibility (assessed at T−6 weeks)\n• Active Emberline work; ≥10 of prior 12 weeks with payroll + job records.\n• Stable crew roster and manager able to attend one 90-min training.\n• No known shutdown, mobilisation or planned long absence during observation.\n• Exclude only for pre-specified safety, data or continuity failure—record reason/date.\nConstrained draw\n1  Freeze candidate list\n2  Block by region (E/Scotland), size/capacity and baseline OT quartile; balance job mix/travel where data allow.\n3  Randomly assign within blocks using seed logged by Mikkel + finance witness.\nIf imbalance >20% on any pre-set factor: re-draw once before access; otherwise pause for sponsor decision.\nMeasurement calendar\n• Baseline: 12 complete weeks immediately before launch (same weekdays).\n• Observation: 12 complete weeks; synchronized Monday–Sunday windows for all groups.\n• Activation delayed >7 days: crew remains assigned; exposure window flagged and ITT retained.\n• Holiday/partial week: keep in panel, mark event; do not silently drop.\nVOLUNTARY PARTICIPATION\nAstrid may invite all eligible crews; crews may decline training or access without penalty. Decliners stay in the allocated group for analysis. Allocation is never based on enthusiasm, manager preference or expected savings.\n\n=== 幻灯片 4 ===\n03 / METRIC, DATA & FIDELITY\nMeasure one primary outcome—and the ways savings can mislead\nCavendish Logistics • Emberline Clinic • planning assumption set • 20 Aug 2026\n04\nPrimary outcome (weekly, crew level)\nOT rate = approved overtime hours ÷ productive paid hours × 100; productive hours = paid hours on Emberline jobs, excluding leave/training.\nAggregate as crew-weighted 12-week mean; report hours and rate. Same payroll coding rules in England/Scotland.\nBalancing / guardrails (secondary)\nOn-time job completion; missed/late jobs; safety incidents; absence; agency hours/cost; travel hours; rework/callbacks; schedule changes \u003c24h; worker pulse (monthly).\nAny material service, safety or cost-shift harm overrides an OT saving.\nRequired capture map\nPayroll/timekeeping\nOT + paid/productive hours; weekly freeze\nJasmin / payroll lead\nDispatch/job ledger\njob volume, mix, urgent work, completion, travel\nAstrid / ops\nTool audit log\nlogin, eligible jobs scheduled, overrides, active weeks\nVendor admin + Mikkel\nPeople/exception log\nabsence, transfers, cross-crew work, disruptions, approver\nCrew managers; Mikkel\nData quality gate: completeness ≥95%, no duplicate crew-week keys, period alignment checked, and OT reconciles to payroll total within 1%. Late/unreliable fields escalate to Mikkel; missingness ≥10% in any group → pause analysis and label inconclusive.\n\n=== 幻灯片 5 ===\n04 / ANALYSIS & DECISION RULES\nPre-declare what “success” means\nCavendish Logistics • Emberline Clinic • planning assumption set • 20 Aug 2026\n05\nPrimary analysis\nIntention-to-treat difference-in-differences on weekly crew panel: (post−baseline OT rate) treatment minus comparison, adjusted for region, capacity, job mix, travel and logged disruptions.\nEstimate = percentage-point and relative change; cluster-robust or randomization-based interval at crew level. With 11 crews, precision is limited; no invented power claim.\nEvidence standard\nBaseline data readiness triggers a simple simulation/precision check before launch. Report 95% interval (or exact randomization interval) and all 11 assignments.\nRegional/crew subgroups are exploratory only; no post-hoc “winner” claim.\nDecision matrix (all conditions must be visible in the signed readout)\nFAVORABLE\n≥10% relative OT-rate reduction AND interval’s upper bound \u003c0% (or exact p\u003c0.10) AND no guardrail breach; supports scoped rollout case, not automatic purchase.\nHARMFUL / UNFAVOURABLE\nOT rate worsens ≥10% OR any material safety/service harm or cost shift; stop/rollback and investigate.\nINCONCLUSIVE\nInterval spans meaningful benefit and null/harm, fidelity \u003c80% of eligible jobs, or missingness ≥10%; do not claim proof either way.\nThe vendor’s 20% is a contextual benchmark only. A raw before/after fall—without the concurrent comparison—is prohibited as the primary success claim.\n\n=== 幻灯片 6 ===\n05 / FIDELITY, WITHDRAWAL & GATES\nExceptions are decided before outcomes are visible\nCavendish Logistics • Emberline Clinic • planning assumption set • 20 Aug 2026\n06\nFidelity & contamination controls\n• Pilot package identical: 90-min training, standard configuration, launch week, helpdesk and weekly check-in.\n• Access is role-based; comparison crews blocked from pilot tenant. Log shared dispatchers, worker transfers, cross-crew jobs and support contacts.\n• Fidelity flag: \u003c80% eligible jobs scheduled in tool for 2 consecutive weeks → Amber review.\nProspective exception rule\n• Crew may withdraw/ be diverted only for safety, client emergency, legal/privacy issue or documented continuity risk.\n• Astrid requests; Mikkel records event, timing, crew, reason, approver and analytical treatment. Camila approves legal/privacy; Eamon approves continuity spend.\n• After launch, a replacement may cover work operationally but never replaces the original crew in primary ITT. Replacement is tagged exploratory only.\nPAUSE / STOP GATES\nImmediate stop: safety incident, privacy/security breach, material service harm, or unauthorized access (Astrid + Camila; Eamon notified). Pause: data completeness \u003c95%, contamination, fidelity \u003c80%, or major co-intervention; Mikkel documents disposition within 2 business days. Resume only with written gate log.\n\n=== 幻灯片 7 ===\n06 / TIMELINE, OWNERS & RECORDS\nRun the pilot to a dated checklist\nCavendish Logistics • Emberline Clinic • planning assumption set • 20 Aug 2026\n07\nW−8 to −6\nDesign + eligibility freeze\nMikkel / Astrid\nSigned protocol; eligible list; privacy notice\nW−6 to −4\nBaseline extract + quality gate\nJasmin + data owner\n12-week panel; reconciliation report\nW−4\nBlocked random draw + balance check\nMikkel + finance witness\nSeed, block table, imbalance decision\nW−3 to −1\nContract, DPIA/monitoring review, access build, training\nCamila + vendor admin\nExecuted terms; RBAC list; attendance\nW0–12\nSynchronized launch, weekly monitoring\nAstrid + crew managers\nTool logs; exception/co-intervention log\nW13–15\nFreeze, clean, model, readout\nMikkel + Jasmin\nLocked dataset; analysis memo; interval\nW16\nFinal decision / records archive\nEamon (sponsor)\nDecision minute; rollout/stop/inconclusive action\nRecord pack: frozen protocol/version, allocation seed and inputs, signed access/notice logs, weekly extracts, exceptions, gate decisions, analysis dataset hash, budget ledger and final decision minute.\n\n=== 幻灯片 8 ===\n07 / ENGLAND–SCOTLAND & CONTRACTING\nCommon controls; local facts still need confirmation\nCavendish Logistics • Emberline Clinic • planning assumption set • 20 Aug 2026\n08\nCOMMON UK / GB BASELINE\n• UK GDPR / Data Protection Act principles: minimisation, purpose, access, retention, breach route.\n• Written pilot scope, security, support, export/deletion and non-production status.\n• Payroll/timekeeping definitions, H&S escalation, equality and worker transparency checks.\n• No individual performance monitoring beyond scheduling purpose without review.\nCONFIRM SEPARATELY\nEngland: local bank-holiday / client-access calendar, workforce consultation route, site rules and records owner.\n\nScotland: Scottish bank-holiday / travel calendar, local workforce arrangements, site/client rules and records owner.\n\nThese are planning checks—not legal conclusions.\nCAMILA’S PRE-ACCESS CHECKLIST\nTerm + crew scope; price basis; exit/no auto-renewal or rollout; pilot acceptance as non-production; data ownership/processor roles; RBAC; sub-processors; incident SLA; support; export/deletion; IP and confidentiality; worker notice; no commitment created by moving beyond pilot.\nCamila confirms unresolved jurisdiction, employment, client-contract and privacy questions in writing before access. Astrid owns local calendar/operations confirmation; Jasmin owns payroll record provenance.\n\n=== 幻灯片 9 ===\n08 / LOW–BASE–HIGH GBP ESTIMATE\nPlanning budget: visible assumptions, not a quote\nCavendish Logistics • Emberline Clinic • planning assumption set • 20 Aug 2026\n09\nAssumption: 11 crews, 12-week observation; vendor pricing unknown, so license is an allowance. Internal rates are loaded planning rates.\nLine item (qty × rate)\nLOW\nBASE\nHIGH\nInternal PM + finance + ops (160/220/300 h × £55)\n£8,800\n£12,100\n£16,500\nVendor license allowance (11 crews × £80/£120/£180 × 3 mo)\n£2,640\n£3,960\n£5,940\nImplementation / integration (40/70/110 h × £85)\n£3,400\n£5,950\n£9,350\nTraining + support (11 × £180/£260/£360)\n£1,980\n£2,860\n£3,960\nData engineering + analysis (60/100/150 h × £75)\n£4,500\n£7,500\n£11,250\nPrivacy / contracting review (20/35/55 h × £150)\n£3,000\n£5,250\n£8,250\nTravel / materials allowance\n£800\n£1,500\n£2,500\nContingency (10% of above)\n£2,512\n£3,912\n£5,775\n£27,632\n£43,032\n£63,525\nTOTAL (sum of visible lines)\nPause at W4: estimated committed £18k/£27k/£39k (license + setup + review + work done); avoidable remainder requires written stop decision. Excludes VAT, production rollout, payroll policy changes, hardware and client compensation.\n\n=== 幻灯片 10 ===\n09 / DECISION REQUEST\nAuthorize only when the record is complete\nCavendish Logistics • Emberline Clinic • planning assumption set • 20 Aug 2026\n10\nEamon’s approval today\n☐ Fund scenario: Low £27,632  /  Base £43,032  /  High £63,525\n☐ Select 12-week window and confirm 5–6 treatment crews\n☐ Name final data owner and finance witness\n☐ Require Camila’s written pre-access confirmation\n☐ Accept that “inconclusive” is a valid result\nBefore any login is issued\n1  Eligibility + baseline quality gate passed\n2  Allocation seed, block table and balance check archived\n3  Contract, privacy/monitoring and local England/Scotland checks signed\n4  RBAC, training, notice and support package ready\n5  Stop/pause contacts tested; record pack location shared\nFinal decision at W16: FAVORABLE / HARMFUL–UNFAVOURABLE / INCONCLUSIVE → documented rollout, rollback, redesign or no-go. Never substitute an unadjusted before–after fall for the comparative readout.","truncated":false,"note":"PPTX 共10页的文字提取；不还原位置、动画和版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The required PowerPoint exists under the exact filename and is a valid, openable PPTX.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Submitted file metadata and package structure.","grader_hint":"Confirm `crew_scheduling_pilot_protocol.pptx` is present at the submission root and can be opened or parsed as a PowerPoint file."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation contains no more than ten slides, including the title slide.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"PowerPoint slide count.","grader_hint":"Count all slides in the package, including title, divider, and appendix slides."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The visible slides are self-contained; no decision rule, definition, exception rule, or required action is available only in speaker notes.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Visible slide content compared with speaker notes.","grader_hint":"Inspect notes if present and withhold credit if a user must read them to operate the protocol."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The protocol frames a testable causal question about whether access to and use of the scheduling tool reduces overtime relative to a credible counterfactual.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Objective, hypothesis, or evaluation question on the slides.","grader_hint":"Look for a comparison-based question, not merely a target to observe lower overtime after launch."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The stated scope identifies the population of eleven Cavendish field crews supporting Emberline Clinic work in England and Scotland.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope or pilot population statement.","grader_hint":"Equivalent wording is acceptable if the crew count, client context, and both jurisdictions are clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The crew is identified as the assignment unit, or a different assignment unit is explicitly justified with a credible plan to prevent within-crew spillover.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Allocation and unit-of-analysis provisions.","grader_hint":"Individual worker assignment without a convincing contamination control does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Eligibility criteria are objective, assessable before allocation, and address operational readiness without selecting crews based on favorable expected outcomes.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Crew eligibility and readiness rules.","grader_hint":"Suitable criteria may include stable payroll capture, minimum operating continuity, training availability, and absence of a known shutdown."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Exclusion criteria and their assessment point are fixed before outcome data are reviewed.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exclusion, lock, or preregistration language.","grader_hint":"The protocol should prevent post-result removal of inconvenient crews."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The protocol separates voluntary engagement or consent from treatment allocation so that volunteering alone does not determine which crews receive the tool.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recruitment, participation, and allocation rules.","grader_hint":"Credit a bounded design that preserves adoption while addressing volunteer bias; a volunteer-users versus non-volunteers comparison is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"A concurrent comparison group is retained for the pilot period.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Pilot design or comparison description.","grader_hint":"Look for crews observed during the same calendar period without pilot access, or an equivalently credible concurrent design."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Allocation accounts prospectively for material crew differences such as region, crew size or capacity, job mix, travel burden, and baseline overtime.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Matching, blocking, stratification, or constrained-allocation provisions.","grader_hint":"The method need not use every named factor, but it must use the most consequential factors and explain any trade-off caused by only eleven crews."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The treatment allocation mechanism is reproducible and protected from managerial cherry-picking.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Randomization, constrained draw, or documented assignment procedure.","grader_hint":"Look for a frozen candidate set, recorded allocation inputs, and either random assignment within balanced groups or another auditable mechanism."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The protocol requires a pre-launch balance check and states what happens if material imbalance is found before access is granted.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Readiness or allocation validation gate.","grader_hint":"Credit only if the response to imbalance is prospective and does not rely on observed pilot outcomes."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"A precise baseline duration and a precise pilot observation duration are stated in weeks or dates.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Timeline or measurement-period definitions.","grader_hint":"Both periods must be operationally definite; phrases such as 'several weeks' are insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The chosen baseline is long enough to characterize ordinary crew-level overtime variation, or the protocol conditions launch on demonstrating that it is adequate.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Baseline rationale and data-readiness gate.","grader_hint":"Assess the rationale rather than requiring one fixed number of weeks."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Seasonality is controlled through concurrent observation and a design or model that does not equate a calendar shift with a tool effect.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Timing, comparison, and analysis provisions.","grader_hint":"A simple prior-period comparison, even over the same number of weeks, is not enough by itself."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Treatment and comparison measurement windows are synchronized, with a rule for partial weeks, holidays, or staggered activation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Measurement calendar and activation rules.","grader_hint":"The protocol should prevent unequal exposure windows from being counted as performance differences."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Software access, shared scheduling support, and cross-crew working are controlled or tracked to detect contamination between pilot and comparison crews.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Access-control and contamination provisions.","grader_hint":"Look for named contamination routes and an actionable record or response."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Pilot crews receive a standardized minimum package of training, launch timing, support, and permitted tool configuration.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Implementation standard or fidelity requirements.","grader_hint":"The protocol should avoid interpreting unequal implementation support as a product effect."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Implementation fidelity is measurable using defined indicators rather than an unsupported assertion that a crew used the tool.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Adoption or fidelity metrics.","grader_hint":"Examples include active scheduling weeks, eligible jobs scheduled, overrides, or authenticated use; exact indicators may vary."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Concurrent operational changes that could affect overtime are held stable where feasible and otherwise logged for analysis.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Change-control and confounder provisions.","grader_hint":"Look for staffing, overtime approval policy, dispatch rules, job allocation, incentives, or similar co-interventions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The exception log captures the event, timing, affected crew and period, reason, approver, and planned analytical treatment.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception and deviation record specification.","grader_hint":"Equivalent fields are acceptable if the record supports an audit of what changed and its effect on interpretation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Withdrawal, urgent diversion, and replacement rules are settled prospectively and resolve the continuity-versus-comparability conflict without using emerging outcomes.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Crew withdrawal, diversion, and replacement protocol.","grader_hint":"Look for timing cutoffs, permitted circumstances, approval authority, and whether a replacement can enter the primary analysis."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The primary analysis preserves crews in their assigned groups using an intention-to-treat principle, or states and justifies an equivalently bias-resistant primary rule.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Primary analysis population definition.","grader_hint":"The exact phrase 'intention-to-treat' is not mandatory if the rule clearly analyzes crews as originally assigned."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The primary overtime outcome has an explicit numerator, denominator or exposure basis, population, time unit, and aggregation level.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Metric definition or formula.","grader_hint":"The metric must be reproducible and should not be only an undefined percentage reduction."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The primary metric adjusts for workload or productive exposure so a reduction caused only by fewer jobs or hours is not mistaken for efficiency.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Primary outcome formula and workload definition.","grader_hint":"Accept overtime per paid, productive, scheduled, or comparable workload unit if the denominator is defensible and consistently available."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Overtime-hour treatment is consistent across jurisdictions and periods, with rules for corrections, premiums, leave, travel, training, and partial pay periods where relevant.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Overtime data definition.","grader_hint":"The document need not dictate payroll law, but it must prevent inconsistent extraction or double counting."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Secondary measures cover operational service and implementation performance without competing with the single primary overtime outcome.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Outcome hierarchy.","grader_hint":"Suitable measures include schedule stability, utilization, travel, rework, or adoption; the primary metric must remain identifiable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Balancing or guardrail measures test whether overtime savings were purchased through poorer service, unsafe work, excess agency use, missed jobs, or shifted cost.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Guardrail measures and decision rules.","grader_hint":"At least one service or quality guardrail and one workforce, safety, or cost-shifting guardrail should be actionable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Required data are mapped to plausible source systems or records, responsible owners, capture frequency, and a freeze or extraction point.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Data plan.","grader_hint":"Expected sources may include payroll/timekeeping, dispatch or job records, software logs, staffing records, and exception logs."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Pre-analysis data-quality checks address completeness, duplicates, crew identifiers, time-period alignment, and reconciliation to an authoritative total.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Data validation or readiness checklist.","grader_hint":"Credit equivalent controls that would reveal materially unreliable overtime or workload data before analysis."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Missing or late data have a prospective handling rule, escalation threshold, and effect on the readiness or interpretation decision.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Missing-data and data-readiness provisions.","grader_hint":"An instruction merely to fill blanks or drop incomplete crews is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The protocol captures major time-varying demand and disruption factors that could explain overtime independently of the tool.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Covariate or event-log requirements.","grader_hint":"Examples include job volume and mix, vacancies, absence, severe weather, holidays, urgent client work, travel disruption, and major projects."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The primary analysis compares change between assigned groups or uses an equivalently credible crew-and-time model rather than only comparing raw post-pilot totals.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Analysis specification.","grader_hint":"Difference-in-differences, a suitably specified panel model, or another transparent concurrent comparative method can satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The analysis reports effect size and uncertainty appropriate to assignment by crew.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Statistical reporting requirements.","grader_hint":"Look for an interval, randomization-based uncertainty, or a clearly justified equivalent that recognizes clustering and the small number of crews."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"A numeric practical-effect threshold and an uncertainty or evidence condition jointly define what movement counts as a favorable signal.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Success or decision rule.","grader_hint":"The threshold may differ from 20%, but it must be explicit, justified, and paired with an evidence condition rather than applied to a raw point estimate alone."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The vendor's 20% claim is treated as a claim to test or contextual benchmark, not as an assumed effect, sole null hypothesis, or automatic pass threshold.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Hypothesis and decision framework.","grader_hint":"Credit an explicit distinction between the vendor claim, the minimum worthwhile effect, and the observed estimate."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The protocol assesses whether eleven crews can provide the desired precision and does not claim unsupported power from invented variance data.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Feasibility, precision, or sample-size discussion.","grader_hint":"A data-readiness calculation using baseline variation, simulation once baseline data exist, or an explicit precision limitation can satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document actively warns that an unadjusted before-and-after reduction cannot establish the tool's effect and prohibits its use as the primary success claim.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Protocol warning, reporting rule, or prohibited-use statement.","grader_hint":"Correct comparative analysis elsewhere is not enough; the common misuse must be explicitly discouraged."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The decision framework separately defines favorable, unfavorable or harmful, and inconclusive outcomes, including the treatment of mixed metric results.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Outcome matrix or decision rules.","grader_hint":"The framework should not force every plausible result into a binary success or failure."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Regional, job-mix, or crew-level subgroup findings are identified as exploratory unless the protocol provides a credible pre-specified basis and adequate information for confirmatory claims.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Subgroup analysis and interpretation rules.","grader_hint":"Look for protection against selecting a favorable subgroup after seeing results."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The timeline sequences preparation, baseline validation, allocation, contracting and access approval, training, launch, monitoring, analysis, and the final decision.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Timeline or roadmap.","grader_hint":"Milestones need concrete dates, weeks, or dependencies sufficient for execution."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Named roles own data readiness, allocation control, operations, training, software access, legal or contracting review, budget control, analysis, exceptions, and the final decision.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Governance or responsibility assignments.","grader_hint":"Roles may be assigned to the named stakeholders or functional owners; every material action must have one accountable owner."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Readiness, pause, and stop gates have objective triggers, an authorized decision-maker, and a documented disposition.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Gate and escalation rules.","grader_hint":"Suitable triggers include unsafe operation, data failure, material service harm, privacy breach, contamination, or inadequate adoption."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The budget covers the full lifecycle rather than only software fees, including internal labor, implementation or integration, training and support, data or analysis work, contingency, and any termination exposure.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Budget categories.","grader_hint":"Equivalent cost groupings are acceptable if material pilot costs are not omitted."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"For each scenario, line costs follow the displayed quantities, rates or allowances, and the total equals the sum of included line items within normal rounding.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Low, base, and high budget tables or calculations.","grader_hint":"Recalculate visible arithmetic. Allow rounding differences of no more than £1 per line and £5 per scenario total unless the deck states a coarser rounding convention."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Low, base, and high estimates vary through coherent stated assumptions, identify exclusions, and distinguish avoidable from committed cost if the pilot pauses or terminates early.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scenario assumptions and cost-exposure analysis.","grader_hint":"The three scenarios must not be unexplained percentage uplifts applied to a single unsupported total."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Pre-access contract protections address pilot term and scope, pricing basis, exit without unintended rollout commitment, data ownership and permitted use, security or breach duties, support, export or deletion, and acceptance of the pilot as non-production.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Contracting checklist or gate.","grader_hint":"Equivalent concise provisions are acceptable; look especially for protection against an automatic subscription or lock-in after the pilot."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Worker and scheduling data controls apply data minimization, role-based access, retention or deletion, notice or transparency, and review of monitoring implications before collection.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Privacy and information-governance provisions.","grader_hint":"The protocol should distinguish operational scheduling data from individual performance monitoring and route any monitoring expansion for review."},{"rubric_item_id":"R50","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The England–Scotland comparison distinguishes requirements that are common across Great Britain or the UK from genuinely local, contractual, workforce, calendar, or operational checks requiring separate confirmation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdiction comparison.","grader_hint":"Credit a careful comparison that avoids manufacturing legal differences and identifies relevant local checks such as policies, consultation arrangements, bank-holiday calendars, client terms, or record ownership."},{"rubric_item_id":"R51","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Legal or jurisdictional statements are framed as implementation assumptions or counsel-confirmation items rather than being attributed to Camila as settled advice.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Legal caveats, owners, and approval gate.","grader_hint":"Look for specific questions and an accountable confirmation point, not a generic disclaimer."},{"rubric_item_id":"R52","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The presentation is internally consistent, legible, decision-ready, and concrete enough that the implementation team can run the pilot without asking the author to resolve a material design choice.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole presentation, including agreement among scope, timeline, budget, metrics, gates, and responsibilities.","grader_hint":"Review the artifact as an operating protocol. Withhold credit for contradictory periods or thresholds, unreadable density, placeholders, orphaned actions, or unresolved choices central to execution."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"Cavendish Logistics is considering a limited pilot of scheduling software for eleven field crews serving Emberline Clinic across England and Scotland. The protocol will govern the pilot and support operational, financial, contracting, and legal review before access is granted.","difficulty_sources":["Designing a credible concurrent comparison across only eleven heterogeneous crews","Separating a tool effect from seasonality, demand, volunteer bias, and operational disruption","Resolving voluntary participation and post-allocation replacement tensions prospectively","Defining decision thresholds and uncertainty without historical variance data","Producing auditable low, base, and high cost estimates without a vendor quotation","Comparing England and Scotland implementation checks without inventing legal differences"],"authoring_plan":{"artifact_intent":"Construct a concise operating protocol that freezes the pilot design before access is granted and remains interpretable under favorable, null, harmful, disrupted, or inconclusive outcomes.","gold_design_constraints":["Use the crew as the assignment unit because workers, dispatch support, and schedules can spill over within a crew.","Retain a concurrent control condition. Use a frozen eligible set and a reproducible blocked, matched, or constrained allocation balancing the most material available pre-pilot factors, including region, capacity, job mix, travel burden, and baseline overtime.","Permit voluntary engagement without allowing volunteering to define treatment status. Separate willingness, training readiness, and data or monitoring notices from allocation.","Specify baseline and observation periods precisely. Keep treatment and comparison crews on synchronized windows and predefine treatment of activation delays, holidays, and partial weeks.","Prevent contamination through access control and records of shared dispatchers, cross-crew jobs, worker transfers, or scheduling support.","Freeze exclusion, diversion, withdrawal, and replacement rules before outcomes are examined. Primary analysis should retain original assignments. A replacement introduced after launch must not silently replace the original crew in the primary estimate.","Standardize training, support, configuration, and launch conditions, and measure implementation fidelity using system or workflow evidence.","Hold overtime approval, job-allocation, staffing, and incentive policies stable where possible. Log unavoidable co-interventions and operational disruptions with timing and affected crews.","Define one primary overtime metric that is normalized for workload or productive exposure and calculated consistently from authoritative timekeeping and job records. Maintain service, safety, workforce, agency-cost, and cost-shifting guardrails.","Use a comparative change model or equivalent panel analysis suitable for crew-level assignment. Report an effect estimate and uncertainty recognizing the small cluster count.","Do not manufacture a conventional power claim without empirical baseline variance. Condition final precision assessment on baseline data and acknowledge that the pilot may produce a useful estimate yet remain inconclusive.","Set a numeric minimum worthwhile effect separately from the vendor's 20% claim. Pair the practical threshold with an uncertainty condition and guardrail requirements.","Provide prospective classifications for favorable, unfavorable or harmful, and inconclusive outcomes. A confidence interval spanning materially beneficial and null or harmful effects must not be labeled proof of no effect.","Explicitly prohibit a raw before-and-after reduction from serving as the primary causal claim.","Treat regional or crew-level subgroup results as exploratory unless pre-specified and supportable.","Include objective readiness, pause, and stop gates for data quality, safety, service harm, privacy or security issues, contamination, and inadequate implementation.","Cover the full cost lifecycle in GBP. Low, base, and high scenario totals must derive from visible assumptions, quantities, rates or allowances, contingency, and termination exposure rather than unsupported percentage scaling.","Require pilot-term, pricing, exit, non-renewal, data-use, data-return or deletion, security, support, and non-production protections before software access.","Apply data minimization, role-based access, retention, transparency, and monitoring-impact review. Do not imply that ordinary scheduling use automatically authorizes individual performance monitoring.","For England and Scotland, distinguish UK- or Great Britain-wide frameworks from local calendars, policies, workforce arrangements, client terms, and other facts requiring separate confirmation. Do not invent a legal divergence merely to populate the comparison.","Assign one accountable owner and a timing point to each gate, data responsibility, exception, approval, budget decision, and final recommendation."],"imperfections_and_tensions_to_preserve":["The vendor claim is unverified and must not be treated as an expected result.","Only eleven heterogeneous crews exist, limiting balance and statistical precision.","Overtime is seasonal and correlated with changing demand and job mix.","Likely volunteers are already better managed, creating selection bias.","Voluntary participation and unbiased allocation are both reasonable requirements that must be reconciled.","Operational continuity favors replacing a diverted crew, while design integrity favors freezing the comparison; the protocol must settle the edge case before outcomes are visible.","No price quote, historical performance, legal conclusion, or variance estimate exists, requiring labeled assumptions and conditional gates.","A familiar but invalid raw before-and-after success claim must be actively discouraged."],"budget_gold_rules":["Use GBP and show low, base, and high scenarios.","For every line item, derive cost from a displayed quantity and rate or from a clearly identified allowance.","Include internal labor, vendor or license allowance, implementation or integration, training and support, data and analysis, contingency, and pause or termination exposure.","Make each scenario total equal the included line items and make scenario differences traceable to stated assumptions.","Distinguish sunk or committed cost from avoidable future cost at relevant gates."],"intended_verdict":"A conforming protocol authorizes a controlled, concurrent, crew-level pilot only after data, contract, privacy, balance, and operational readiness gates pass. It does not promise that eleven crews can validate the vendor's 20% claim, and it allows the final verdict to be favorable, unfavorable or harmful, or inconclusive based on a predeclared practical threshold, uncertainty, fidelity, and guardrails."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"mixed_media_bundle","structure":"freeform"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"pilot_protocol","filename":"crew_scheduling_pilot_protocol.pptx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.presentationml.presentation","description":"A decision-ready pilot design protocol and low/base/high planning budget for the crew-scheduling tool.","required":true,"constraints":["Write the file at the top level of the working directory using exactly the specified filename.","Use no more than ten slides, including the title slide.","All critical content must be visible on the slides rather than confined to speaker notes.","Show budget scenarios in GBP.","Use labeled planning assumptions and do not invent historical performance, vendor quotations, or legal advice."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"plan_work","work_product":"budget_forecast","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"project_management_specialists","sector":"professional_scientific_technical_services","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":10.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"domain_expert","reviewer_policy":"The reference solution should be reviewed independently by a project controls or evaluation-design practitioner and by a contracting, privacy, or employment-risk reviewer. Reviewers should check causal interpretability, operational feasibility, budget arithmetic, jurisdictional restraint, and whether all instructions fit visibly within ten slides.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No historical crew-level variance, workload, payroll, vendor pricing, or implementation data are supplied, so the reference solution must use clearly labeled planning assumptions and a baseline data-readiness gate rather than claim validated statistical power.","The protocol can identify legal and contracting confirmation points but cannot substitute for advice based on Cavendish policies, workforce arrangements, the vendor contract, or Emberline Clinic terms."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:45:00+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:45:00+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:45:00+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:45:00+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":false,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"instruction 与 rubric criterion 存在 8-gram 逐字重叠: ['R02', 'R05']","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:45:00+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_b1/runs/2767756a35509b1a","declared_deliverables":["crew_scheduling_pilot_protocol.pptx"]};
